c57z9 xr9ek0c xisnujt" data-testid="cell-frame-secondary"><div class="xjbqb8w x1iyjqo2 x1f6kntn x1fc57z9 x6ikm8r x10wlt62 x1yc453h xlyipyv xuxw1ft xo1l8bm"><span class="x78zum5 x1cy8zhl" title="‪🤖 Task 2 Complete: California Housing — Model Comparison &amp; Optimization

Following up on my last post about building a baseline Linear Regression model — here&#39;s how I took it further as part of the Artificial Intelligence &amp; Machine Learning Internship at Maincrafts Technology!

In Task 1, I built a single model. Task 2 pushed me to think like an ML engineer actually does in real-world projects — preparing data properly, testing multiple algorithms, and choosing the best one based on evidence, not guesswork.

🔍 What I did:
✅ Applied Feature Scaling (StandardScaler) to bring all features to a common scale
✅ Trained and compared 3 regression models: Linear Regression, Ridge Regression, and Decision Tree
✅ Evaluated each model using RMSE and R² Score
✅ Selected the best-performing model based on measurable metrics
✅ Visualized Actual vs Predicted results for the winning model

💡 Key Insights:
• Feature scaling was critical — without it, features like Population (range: 3–35,682) would have unfairly dominated smaller-scale features like AveRooms
• Ridge Regression&#39;s regularization helped control overfitting by penalizing large coefficients
• Decision Tree captured non-linear patterns that linear models missed, but needed depth control to stay generalizable

This project taught me that real ML workflows aren&#39;t about training one model and stopping — they&#39;re about comparing, questioning, and justifying decisions with data. A mindset shift I&#39;ll carry into every future project.

Grateful to Maincrafts Technology for this hands-on learning experience! 🚀

🔗 Full project &amp; code: https://github.com/khushisingh916228-droid/California-Housing-Model-Comparison

